        "France's school year runs September to early July, and a DP2 cohort sitting the May session works internal assessment deadlines, TOK components and the Extended Essay into the winter and early spring — typically November through March — often while BFI or Bac-track siblings in the same household are managing contrôle continu deadlines and Grand Oral preparation on an entirely different calendar. An IB DP tutor working with families in France plans backward from a school's own actual dates rather than a generic syllabus timetable.",
        "Schools set and mark internal assessments themselves before sending them for external moderation, and how much each one counts depends on the subject — often a fifth to a third of the total grade in Mathematics and the sciences, and noticeably more in languages, the arts and performance subjects. A history student needs sources they can genuinely get hold of; a Mathematics student needs a question small enough to actually finish; a science student needs a variable they can control in reality, not just on paper. Most of the tutoring value here is diagnostic — sitting with the actual marking criteria next to a child's draft and pointing at precisely where the marks are being lost.",
        "The Extended Essay runs to 4,000 independent words, guided by a school supervisor across a handful of scheduled check-ins; TOK adds an exhibition plus a 1,600-word essay written to one of the IB's set titles. Neither rewards a late scramble — what matters early is a question that real, reachable sources can actually answer, and a working bibliography assembled well before the halfway point of the school year. A francophone research topic often gives a student in France unusually direct access to primary material, which a tutor can help shape into something sharper than a more generic choice would allow.",
        "None of this bends on academic honesty, and the IB's own checks make sure of it. Whatever gets submitted for an IA, the Extended Essay or TOK has to be the student's own, signed off by both student and supervisor, and screened for similarity against other sources. A tutor's job is to point out a criterion that's been misunderstood, press on a weak piece of reasoning, and demonstrate technique on a comparable but different problem — never the one actually being assessed. Any tutor who offers to draft or substantially rewrite a submission should be dropped immediately.",
        "Because France divides school holidays into three zones — A, B and C — for scheduling purposes, with Paris typically in Zone C, Lyon in Zone A and the Nice-Provence region in Zone B, exam-season revision blocks sometimes need to work around a break that lands on a different week for a Lyon family than for a Paris one. Confirming your school's zone and holiday dates before booking an intensive block avoids a plan built around the wrong calendar.",
