        "Every booking runs on Central European Time, not on tutor-side hours — since France sits in a single zone, a 6 pm CET slot works identically for a family in Paris, Lyon or Nice, which simplifies scheduling compared with a multi-zone country. Tutors are based in India, and CET evenings translate to a specific India-side working window that tutors are rostered around, so the same weekly slot holds consistently rather than drifting.",
        "An opening lesson is used to size up the problem rather than to teach content outright. The tutor asks what the current term's assessment actually covers, watches the student talk through two or three problems out loud, and pins down where the thinking itself goes wrong rather than just where an answer does. From there, sessions of 60 minutes suit routine upkeep and 90 minutes suit internal-assessment or past-paper work, usually weekly or twice-weekly, stepping up around mocks or the written exam period.",
        "A shared, interactive whiteboard on the video call means both tutor and student are writing on the same surface, in view of each other, rather than describing work over a static screen. Graphing and equation tools handle the Sciences and Mathematics side directly; a tracked-comment document works better for essay and literature subjects, which is a natural fit for BFI literature preparation and IB English A alike. Past-paper annotation happens live, and everything gets saved somewhere the family can find it again later.",
